# Split an over-large issue (`/iflow-split`)

`/iflow-split` cuts **one over-large GitHub issue** into 2–5 flat child issues, links each as a GitHub native sub-issue of the parent, appends a parent task list, parks the parent locally as a tracker, and optionally starts the first child.

Use it when an existing issue is too big for one PR but is **not** a multi-stage epic. Staged work with dependencies belongs to `/iflow-epic`. Creating one new issue belongs to `/iflow-issue`.

This is an **off-path** command — the lifecycle dispatcher (`/iflow`) never auto-runs it, because it creates GitHub issues and parent/child links. Invoke it explicitly.

## How it relates to other commands

- **`/iflow-issue`** — one new well-specified issue. Split operates on an *existing* parent.
- **`/iflow-epic`** — staged specs + `publish`. If the cut needs stages or `Depends on`, stop and use epic instead.
- **`/iflow-pick` / `/iflow-plan`** — may *offer* this command when an issue looks over-large; they never create children themselves.

